One common way to introduce vulnerabilities is through **Insecure Deserialization**, where an application takes untrusted data from a user (like a cookie or a POST parameter) and attempts to reconstruct it into a Java object without proper validation. This can lead to Remote Code Execution (RCE) if a malicious object is sent to the application.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.Base64;
import javax.servlet.http.*;

public class DeserializationHandler extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
        // This method is vulnerable to insecure deserialization.
        // It reads a Base64 encoded object from a request parameter and attempts to deserialize it 
        // without any integrity checks, allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary code.
        String encodedData = request.getParameter("userData");
        byte[] decodedBytes = Base64.getDecoder().decode(encodedData);

        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(decodedBytes))) {
            // Deserializing untrusted data directly creates a high-severity vulnerability.
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

